{"job": "N01-baseline", "calls": 3, "questions_counted": 2, "closed": false, "failure": null, "response": {"question": {"text": "When someone reserves a slot, do you need a way to contact them, such as an email address or phone number? Or is it enough that they give a name or nickname and check the public notice for any changes?", "decision": "This decides what personal data the system holds. A name or nickname with a private cancel code, plus the public notice, is the smallest and safest setup. Collecting email or phone adds privacy duties and the risk of a data leak. It also affects whether an off-the-shelf booking tool will do, or whether a small custom build is needed."}, "owner_view": "Your new requirements are that visitors can reserve one of 12 telescope time slots and cancel their own reservation, and that names are not published. I'm treating the public ON/OFF notice as staying. I'm also treating these exclusions as still in force: no payments, no mailing lists and no visitor analytics. I read "public cancellation notice" as the public notice about whether the night is going ahead. Tell me if you meant something else.

