r"], "data_access": ["Reservation holds some identifier (name or nickname) visible only to the owner, never published (names unpublished is owner-confirmed)", "Contact details (email or phone) are an open decision. The default proposal is not to collect them.", "Cancellation needs ownership proof. Proposal: private code or link, no visitor accounts.", "Only the owner (and any delegate they name) can see bookings or edit the status", "Third-party tools may store visitor data or add tracking. Check this against the no-analytics and no-mailing-list exclusions before choosing.", "No credentials or private documents requested"], "risk": {"level": "medium", "reasons": ["Now stores personal data (names), so a leak or accidentally public names would be a privacy failure", "Cancellation without accounts can be misused if codes are guessable or shared", "Off-the-shelf tools may show names or track visitors by default, against the owner's exclusions", "If the night is called off, booked visitors can only be told via the public notice unless contact details are collected", "Late go/no-go changes and a single decision-maker risk wasted trips", "Cost, audience size and location are unknown"]}, "recommendation": {"summary": "Proposed, not owner-confirmed: first check whether an existing booking or sign-up tool can meet the requirements (12 slots, self-cancel, names hidden from the public, no payments, no mailing list, no analytics). Pair it with the manually edited public status notice. Consider a small custom build only if no tool fits.", "rationale": ["Slot booking with self-cancel is a common feature, so a tool may avoid custom work and its maintenance", "The exclusions and the hidden-names rule are the likely deal-breakers for off-the-shelf tools and need checking", "A small custom build gives control over data and tracking, but adds cost, maintenance and security responsibility", "Minimising collected data (no contact details by default) lowers privacy risk"], "alternatives":